563. Design a public Java library API.


564. Minimize public surface.


565. Use package-private implementation details.


566. Use final to protect invariants.


567. Use sealed types for closed domain models.


568. Use concurrency modifiers based on actual synchronization requirements.



Traps

569. "Make everything public for convenience."


570. "Make everything final."


571. "Use volatile for thread safety."


572. "Use synchronized everywhere."


573. "Make every class sealed."


574. "Use static for shared state."



Explain why each approach is dangerous.

Design

575. Design a production Order model.


576. Design a thread-safe OrderProcessor.


577. Design a public API with internal implementation classes.


578. Design a sealed order-state hierarchy.


579. Design immutable configuration.



Debugging Challenges

580. A production service has a race condition. Determine whether volatile or synchronization is required.


581. A test suite passes individually but fails collectively. Investigate static state.


582. A library user cannot extend a class. Investigate final/sealed.


583. A module cannot access a public class. Investigate JPMS/export/access.



Production

584. How do modifiers communicate architectural intent?


585. How do modifiers communicate concurrency guarantees?


586. How do modifiers protect invariants?


587. How do modifiers affect backward compatibility?


588. How do modifiers affect framework integration?


589. How do modifiers affect performance?


590. How do you review modifier choices in a production pull request?




