1.7.21 Integrated Modifier Challenge

Build:

OrderSystem
│
├── Order
├── OrderRepository
├── OrderProcessor
├── OrderState
│    ├── Created
│    ├── Paid
│    ├── Shipped
│    └── Cancelled
└── Configuration

Apply:

private
final
static
sealed
permits
non-sealed
synchronized
volatile
transient

Basic

591. Add appropriate modifiers.


592. Explain what each modifier does.



Intermediate

593. Make Order immutable where appropriate.


594. Make configuration safely published.


595. Protect shared processing state.



Advanced

596. Make OrderState a sealed hierarchy.


597. Decide which subclasses are final/non-sealed.


598. Design the concurrency mechanism.



Traps

599. Introduce volatile count++.


600. Introduce mutable static state.


601. Make the entire class public.


602. Add an illegal modifier combination.


603. Introduce an incorrect sealed hierarchy.



Design

604. Review every modifier.


605. Remove unnecessary modifiers.


606. Explain why each remaining modifier exists.



Debugging Challenges

607. Diagnose the intentionally introduced race.


608. Diagnose the static-state test pollution.


609. Diagnose the sealed hierarchy error.


610. Diagnose the access-control error.



Production

611. Perform a final API review.


612. Perform a concurrency review.


613. Perform an extensibility review.


614. Perform a serialization review.


615. Explain which modifiers create long-term API commitments.




---

🔥 1.7.22 Final Mastery Test

Round 1 — Access Control

Without notes, explain:

public
protected
package-private
private

Then answer:

> What can a class in the same package access? What can a subclass in another package access? What can an unrelated class access?




---

Round 2 — final

You should be able to distinguish:

final variable
final reference
final field
final method
final class
effectively final
immutable object

Especially:

> final reference ≠ immutable object




---

Round 3 — static

Explain:

static field
static method
static initialization
static method hiding
static nested class
mutable static state

Then answer:

> Why is static method hiding not runtime polymorphism?




---

Round 4 — Abstraction

Compare:

abstract class
abstract method
interface
default method
final class
sealed class
non-sealed class

You should be able to decide which one belongs in a production design and why.


---

Round 5 — Concurrency

Explain:

synchronized
      ↓
mutual exclusion + visibility

volatile
      ↓
visibility + ordering
      ↓
NOT general atomicity

Then explain why:

volatile int count;
count++;

is still unsafe.


---

Round 6 — Sealed Types

Design:

Payment
├── Card
├── UPI
├── Wallet
└── Cash

using:

sealed
permits
final
non-sealed

and explain why you selected each modifier.


---

Round 7 — Modifier Traps

Explain why these are problematic/illegal where applicable:

abstract + final
abstract method + final
abstract method + private
static + abstract
volatile + incorrect compound operations
public + unnecessary API exposure
final reference + mutable object
static + mutable global state


---

🏆 Module 1.7 Completion Standard

The source's overall mastery definition is:

> Explain → Implement → Explain compiler/JVM behavior → Handle edge cases → Discuss trade-offs → Debug it → Use it in production. 



For 1.7 specifically, don't consider yourself done until you can:

[ ] Explain all four access levels.

[ ] Test access across packages.

[ ] Explain protected cross-package behavior.

[ ] Explain public under JPMS.

[ ] Design deliberate API exposure.

[ ] Explain final for fields, methods and classes.

[ ] Distinguish final references from immutable objects.

[ ] Explain blank final fields.

[ ] Explain effectively final.

[ ] Explain final-field JMM semantics.

[ ] Explain static state and behavior.

[ ] Explain static method hiding.

[ ] Explain static initialization.

[ ] Diagnose mutable static-state problems.

[ ] Explain abstract classes/methods.

[ ] Explain default interface methods.

[ ] Resolve default-method conflicts.

[ ] Explain synchronized and monitors.

[ ] Explain synchronized happens-before.

[ ] Diagnose deadlocks.

[ ] Explain volatile visibility and ordering.

[ ] Explain why volatile doesn't provide general atomicity.

[ ] Diagnose visibility bugs.

[ ] Explain transient.

[ ] Understand the native/JNI boundary.

[ ] Understand the modern relevance of strictfp.

[ ] Design sealed hierarchies.

[ ] Understand non-sealed.

[ ] Understand permits.

[ ] Identify illegal modifier combinations.

[ ] Understand modifier/JMM interactions.

[ ] Make production modifier decisions.

[ ] Debug modifier-related failures.

[ ] Defend modifier choices in a senior-level review.


The master syllabus itself lists exactly these 1.7 areas: access modifiers, final, static, abstract/default, synchronized, volatile, transient, native, strictfp, sealed types, modifier interactions, JMM implications and production decisions. 

⭐ The highest-value 1.7 questions

If you want the questions that separate syntax knowledge from real Java mastery, prioritize these:

1. protected across package boundaries — exactly what is accessible?


2. Why doesn't final make an object immutable?


3. Static method hiding vs runtime overriding?


4. Why can't abstract and final coexist?


5. Default-method conflict resolution?


6. What exactly does synchronized lock?


7. Instance synchronized vs static synchronized?


8. What does synchronized provide beyond mutual exclusion?


9. Why doesn't volatile count++ work safely?


10. Volatile vs synchronized vs AtomicInteger?


11. What does transient actually guarantee?


12. Why is JNI dangerous at the production boundary?


13. Why is strictfp largely historical in modern Java?


14. Sealed vs final vs non-sealed?


15. When should a production class be public, package-private, final, abstract or sealed?



That is the 1.7 pattern we should continue using for 1.8 and 1.9: every individual subtopic gets the complete Basic → Intermediate → Advanced → Traps → Design → Debugging → Production progression, with the source's actual topics preserved rather than collapsing them into a generic interview question bank.
